Range, sensitivity, loading, bandwidth, and response time (@met-response: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #source "../../quantitative-literacy/foundations/Functions-and-tables.lingua#r_0F4KMKK6ET8T22RG617S1RDZS2", #metrology "Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH", #industrial-gases-and-vacuum "../../../utilities/industrial-gases-vacuum-and-high-purity-supplies/measurement/Force-based-gauges-and-the-reference-side.lingua#r_40SN2CZ53BHYP5NCHPEHTXCDSV", #clean-manufacturing "../../../production/clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control/aerosol-measurement/Optical-pulses-are-responses-to-particles.lingua#r_4X97M45Z8TP1PK690KB3ZF9CQV") { r_07SCGP3DGSTJ27T288T3G50J8H
**Read:** [Developing and using measurement models — JCGM GUM-6](../sources/GUM-Models.lingua#r_3AAJPGTHAAF72R05SJ1VJ7HN46), clauses 7, 9–10 and the time-dependent model discussion; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), sections 4–7 and E7 Thermistor Calibration; [Functions, tables, domains, and inverse questions](../../quantitative-literacy/foundations/Functions-and-tables.lingua#r_0F4KMKK6ET8T22RG617S1RDZS2), the whole Record and its assigned preparation.

A measuring system transforms a quantity into indications. A static model I = ax + b describes sensitivity and offset over a stated range; it does not automatically describe response to changing inputs. Saturation, hysteresis, nonlinearity, drift, and loading can remain hidden when only a few points are inspected.

A simple dynamic model τ dI/dt + I = Kx relates indication to input under assumptions. For a step input and first-order response, the indicated change approaches its final value as 1 − exp(−t/τ). At t = τ it has reached about 63.2% of the final change, not completed the measurement. Reading too early can produce a bias that repetition at the same delay will not remove.

The instrument can change what it measures. A probe exchanges heat with an object; a voltmeter draws current through a finite input resistance; a contact probe applies force. The relevant measurand may be the unperturbed quantity or the quantity under the specified measurement procedure. State which model is intended.

**Work:** use a hypothetical first-order response with τ = 5 s and calculate the remaining response difference at 5, 15, and 25 s. Determine a waiting criterion relative to an allowed contribution instead of declaring “three time constants” universally adequate. Sketch a static range with saturation and show why its inverse cannot uniquely recover an out-of-range input.

Connect a device specification to the intended signal: range, bandwidth, input loading, and environmental conditions must agree. A high digit count can coexist with inadequate bandwidth or a changing specimen. Detailed sensor design belongs in the relevant instrumentation discipline; this Record establishes the shared questions a calibration and uncertainty model must answer.

